    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/17023": {
      "title": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Research Associate III – Coastal Hazards Modeling &amp; GIS",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Research Associate III – Coastal Hazards Modeling &amp; GIS Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm Research Associate III – Coastal Hazards Modeling & GIS Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please attach the following 3 (PDF) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) CV (#3) Other Document : 2-3 representative samples of one or more samples of GIS work, python code, or other relevant work products (large projects should be represented by excerpts). (as one complete document upload). Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number SF02203 Job Title Research Associate III – Coastal Hazards Modeling & GIS Position Number PS#00008195 FTE 1.00 FLSA Exempt Position Type Professional Staff Union PSA/NEA - Professional Staff Assoc Pay Grade Level Grade Level: 11 Pay Grade Range Salary Hiring Range:  $70,000 - $80,000 Status Calendar Year, Full-time, Limited Department Information Department Marine Affairs Contact(s) Please note: Job applications must be submitted directly online only at: (https://jobs.uri.edu) Contact Phone/Extension Contact Email Physical Demands Campus Location Kingston Grant Funded Yes Extension Contingency Notes Position is full-time calendar year, limited to 01/08/2028 with possible renewal. ___________________________________________________________________________________ Job Description Summary Search will be open until filled. First consideration will be given to applications received by 09/30/2026. Applications received after 09/30/2026 may be reviewed depending on search progress and needs but are not guaranteed full consideration. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ About URI : The University of Rhode Island enrolls approximately 17,000 students across its graduate and undergraduate programs and is the State’s flagship public research university, as well as the land grant and sea grant university, for the state of Rhode Island. The main campus is located in the historic village of Kingston, and the Bay Campus is located in Narragansett. Both campuses are near major beaches in a beautiful coastal community. URI is just 30 minutes from Providence, RI and within easy reach of Newport, Boston, and New York City. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ THE OPPORTUNITY FOR A HYBRID WORK SCHEDULE IS POSSIBLE AFTER SIX MONTHS . POSITION SUMMARY : The Research Associate III will provide advanced technical support for natural hazards decision support tools at the University of Rhode Island. URI has dedicated significant expertise and resources to develop a suite of tools, including the Coastal Hazards Analysis, Modeling and Prediction system ( CHAMP ), STORMTOOLS , and MyCoast: Rhode Island, which play key roles in addressing storm risks and predicting the impacts of sea level rise and increasing wind and precipitation. CHAMP is an interdisciplinary system developed and maintained by a team of 10-15 researchers and staff spanning Marine Affairs, Ocean Engineering, Oceanography, and RI Sea Grant. The system is in active operational use at the Rhode Island Emergency Management Agency and supports ongoing projects with the U.S. Coast Guard and the Rhode Island Department of Transportation. This position will support applied coastal hazards research that leverages numerical storm and compound flood modeling (rainfall-driven and wind-driven) and geospatial decision-support systems to advance the goals of the Rhode Island Natural Hazards Collaborative, and will serve as the operational and technical lead for CHAMP . The CHAMP team is actively exploring opportunities to integrate AI and machine learning tools into system development, operations, and decision-support products. Candidates with relevant experience or interest in this area are encouraged to discuss it in their application materials. Duties and Responsibilities KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Under the supervision of Austin Becker, Principal Investigator in the College of the Environment and Life Sciences, the Research Associate III will lead the ongoing operation of coastal storm models and application of geospatial information systems. The position focuses on developing and transitioning science-based modeling and GIS tools into reliable, user-focused operational decision-support products for planning, management, and public communication, with primary emphasis on the Coastal Hazards Analysis, Modeling, and Prediction System (see www. RICHAMP .org ). Although work will begin with operating and maintaining existing systems, there is wide opportunity for improvement and innovation, even using alternatives to Esri systems. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : • Operate existing applied workflows and maintain dashboards during initial work period. • Develop, maintain, and enhance advanced GIS products, including interactive dashboards, spatial analysis tools, and map-based decision-support products. • Use GIS tools to acquire, process, and manage hydrological and land-use datasets in support of environmental and flood modeling applications. • Produce GIS -based risk and vulnerability reports to support long-term planning, research, and stakeholder needs. • Design, implement, and manage GIS systems that support tiered user access and varying security and permission levels. • Run and translate model outputs into accessible, user-driven GIS products for planners, emergency managers, researchers, and the public. • Coordinate with Environmental Data Center staff to support data management, system performance, hosting, and long-term reliability of GIS infrastructure. • Support real-time ADCIRC model deployment during storm events, including hurricanes and nor’easters. This requires availability on short notice, including during evenings, weekends, and vacation periods, and is a core expectation of the position. • Contribute to grant writing and proposal development in support of funded projects, including identifying funding opportunities and assisting with technical narratives. • Contribute to outreach products, briefings, and presentations associated with the RI Natural Hazards Tools Collaborative. • Collaborate with investigators and researchers to integrate modeling outputs with observational data, including real-time data streams where appropriate. • Assist in the preparation of technical documentation, reports, peer-reviewed papers, and modeling-related deliverables for funded projects. The following responsibilities may become a significant component of the role depending on the candidate’s background. Candidates with experience in numerical coastal or inland flood modeling are strongly encouraged to apply. • Work with the modeling team to develop, implement, and maintain numerical inland and coastal storm models, with emphasis on GIS -based HEC systems ( HEC - RAS and HEC - HMS ) and ADCIRC / SWAN and related hydrological and hydrodynamic modeling frameworks. • Support model setup, execution, calibration, validation, and post-processing for applied inland and coastal hazards research and decision support. • Lead operational workflows for CHAMP modeling systems, including documentation, version control, and continuity planning. OTHER DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Perform additional duties as required. LICENSES , TOOLS , AND EQUIPMENT : Computers, printers; geographic information system ( GIS ), word processing, data management, spreadsheet software. Must provide own transportation. Required Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED : 1. Master’s degree in GIS , engineering, oceanography, environmental science, geosciences, computer science, or a closely related field. 2. Three or more years of professional experience in GIS or a related field. 3. Advanced knowledge of the Esri ArcGIS platform, including ArcGIS Pro and ArcGIS Online or Enterprise environments. 4. Demonstrated experience with Esri map series tools, model builder, coding or scripting in Python, in support of GIS or modeling workflows. 5. Experience deploying model outputs through web-based or interactive visualization tools. 6. Strong analytical, organizational, and written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications PREFERRED : 1. Experience using Jupyter Notebook and/or Linux-based HPC environments, including job scheduling, scripting, and running large-scale environmental models. 2. Demonstrated experience with compound flooding models integrating hydrologic, hydraulic, and coastal processes (e.g., HEC - HMS / RAS , ADCIRC / SWAN ). 3. Experience developing interactive dashboards, web-based GIS applications, and automated data pipelines. 4. Experience contributing to grant proposals or technical narratives for funded research projects. 5. Experience with or interest in applying AI or machine learning tools to environmental modeling or geospatial decision-support applications. 6. Strong interest in natural hazards resilience and its application to planning, emergency management, and public decision-making. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ EEO Statement: URI is unwavering in its commitment to equal opportunity, community, equity, and diversity. In furtherance of its mission to serve and support all learners as the state’s public flagship university, URI continues to expand efforts to recruit and retain a broadly representative workforce as well as to regularly evaluate the effectiveness of those efforts. All employment decisions, including hiring, are made without regard to protected status. Qualified individuals with disabilities may request reasonable accommodation by contacting the URI benefits office at 401-874-2416. For TTY assistance, contact R.I. Relay Services at 711. Environmental Conditions ENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS : The incumbent is not substantially exposed to adverse environmental conditions. Posting Date 09/02/2026 Closing Date Special Instructions to Applicants Please attach the following 3 ( PDF ) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) CV (#3) Other Document : 2-3 representative samples of one or more samples of GIS work, python code, or other relevant work products (large projects should be represented by excerpts). (as one complete document upload). Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/17018 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Other Document Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a Master's degree in GIS, engineering, oceanography, environmental science, geosciences, computer science, or a closely related field? Yes No * Do you have three or more years of professional experience in GIS or a related field? Yes No * Do you have advanced knowledge of the Esri ArcGIS platform, including ArcGIS Pro and ArcGIS Online or Enterprise environments? Yes No * Do you have demonstrated experience with Esri map series tools, model builder, coding or scripting in Python, in support of GIS or modeling workflows? Yes No * Do you have experience deploying model outputs through web-based or interactive visualization tools? Yes No * Do you have strong analytical, organizational, and written and oral communication skills? Yes No * Are you a URI employee currently in a position covered by the PSA Union? (You will not be disqualified for answering \"No\") Yes No How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Other Chronicle Academic Careers Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed New England Herc The Edu Ledger LinkedIn Asiana In Higher Ed Disable in High ed Blacks In Higher Ed Diversity Jobs HBCU Connect Hispanic in Higher ed LGBT in Higher Ed Native American in Higher ed Woman and Higher Ed Veterans in Higher Edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
